# RUN: midenc --emit=masm=- -o %t/out.masp %S/../../fixtures/masm/cross_module_advice_taint/miden-project.toml 2>&1 | filecheck %s
#
# `--emit` reaching a project build at all is new: the compilation a manifest input used to
# drive reset `output_types` for every target, so this flag was a silent no-op here. The route
# now renders what it publishes, through the `masm` frontend's own artifact declaration.
#
# What this pins that no unit test can: the *whole* composition -- a real `.masm`-rooted
# manifest, the real MASM frontend, the real renderer, and a real `--emit` destination. The
# crate's own tests cover the wiring with a stub frontend and the renderer by calling it
# directly, so a break in how the two meet would pass both.
#
# This is deliberately a **full build**, not `-Canalyze-only`. The `masm` route publishes the
# very same `ProjectSourceInputs` at both `masm.parsed` and `masm.lowered`, so declaring the
# renderer at both -- the obvious mistake, and the one the CHECK-NOTs below exist for -- prints
# every module twice. `-Canalyze-only` stops the build before `masm.lowered` is ever reached,
# so it could not observe that at all. `-o %t/out.masp` keeps the assembled package out of the
# source tree; it overrides only the final artifact, so `--emit=masm=-` still reaches stdout.
#
# The target's namespace is `example`, from the package name, and it is *synthesized* -- neither
# source file declares one. So the `namespace` lines below appear only in emitted Miden
# Assembly, which is what lets the CHECK-NOTs mean "emitted once" rather than "mentioned once".

# Plain CHECK rather than CHECK-LABEL throughout: CHECK-LABEL partitions the input into
# blocks, which would confine the trailing CHECK-NOTs to the last block and let a second copy
# of the whole module tree through. Ordinary CHECKs keep them scoped to "everything after the
# last match", which is what "exactly once" needs.

# Support modules are written first, then the root, matching the standalone `.masm` path.
CHECK: namespace example::support
CHECK: pub proc dirty

# `push.1` rather than the source's `push.FOO`: what is written is the parsed artifact, not a
# copy of the file it came from.
CHECK: push.1

CHECK: namespace example
CHECK: pub mod support
CHECK: pub proc entry

# Exactly once, not twice: these cover the whole remainder of the output.
CHECK-NOT: namespace example::support
CHECK-NOT: pub mod support
